🌳 HashMap vs TreeMap in Java (Focus: Ordering)

| Feature                  | **HashMap**                                      | **TreeMap**                                         |
| ------------------------ | ------------------------------------------------ | --------------------------------------------------- |
| **Ordering**             | ❌ Unordered / unsorted                           | ✅ Sorted by **keys** (natural or `Comparator`)     |
| **Underlying Structure** | Hash table (buckets + `hashCode`/`equals`)       | Red-Black Tree (balanced BST)                       |
| **Null Keys**            | ✅ One null key                                   | ❌ No null keys (with natural ordering)             |
| **Null Values**          | ✅ Allowed                                        | ✅ Allowed                                          |
| **Performance**          | Average `O(1)` get/put                           | `O(log n)` get/put                                  |
| **Key requirement**      | Good `hashCode`/`equals`                         | Keys must be `Comparable` **or** provide Comparator |
| **Thread-safe?**         | ❌ No                                             | ❌ No                                                |
| **Implements**           | `Map`                                            | `NavigableMap` → `SortedMap` → `Map`                |
| **Best for**             | Fast lookup when order doesn’t matter            | Sorted keys / range queries                           |

────────────────────────────────────────
✅ HashMap (no guaranteed order)

```java
Map<String, Integer> hashMap = new HashMap<>();
hashMap.put("Banana", 1);
hashMap.put("Apple", 2);
hashMap.put("Mango", 3);

System.out.println(hashMap);
// Order not guaranteed, e.g. {Mango=3, Apple=2, Banana=1}
```

────────────────────────────────────────
✅ TreeMap (sorted by keys)

```java
Map<String, Integer> treeMap = new TreeMap<>();
treeMap.put("Banana", 1);
treeMap.put("Apple", 2);
treeMap.put("Mango", 3);

System.out.println(treeMap);
// Sorted by key: {Apple=2, Banana=1, Mango=3}

treeMap.lowerKey("Mango"); // key just less than "Mango" → "Banana"
// Also: higherKey, floorKey, ceilingKey, subMap, headMap, tailMap
```

Custom sort:

```java
Map<String, Integer> byLength = new TreeMap<>(Comparator.comparingInt(String::length));
```

────────────────────────────────────────
🧠 Quick decision
- Need **speed**, order irrelevant → `HashMap`
- Need **insertion order** → `LinkedHashMap` (not TreeMap)
- Need **sorted keys / ranges** → `TreeMap`

────────────────────────────────────────
🎯 Summary

| Use **HashMap** when...           | Use **TreeMap** when...                     |
| --------------------------------- | ------------------------------------------- |
| Fast performance matters          | Sorted keys required                        |
| You don’t care about key order    | Natural or custom key order needed          |
| Null key is OK                    | Null keys not allowed                       |
| Simple lookup cache               | Range ops (`subMap`, `headMap`, etc.)       |

🎤 Quick interview traps
1) TreeMap sorts by **keys**, not values.
2) Insertion order ≠ sorted order — insertion order is `LinkedHashMap`.
3) `treeMap.put(null, 1)` → `NullPointerException` (natural ordering).
4) Keys in TreeMap must be mutually comparable (or use a Comparator).
5) Neither HashMap nor TreeMap is thread-safe — use ConcurrentHashMap / sync wrappers if needed.
